  "result": "I implemented `satisfies(version, range)` in `src/semver.js` following `docs/SPEC.md`, and `npm test` passes (4 tests, 0 failed).\n\nIt follows the spec's version format, ordering, range syntax and prerelease rule. It returns `false` for any invalid version or range, including non-string inputs, and never throws.\n\nWhere the spec doesn't say, I made these calls:\n- **Wildcards:** `1.x.3`, `*.2` and `1.2.x-beta` are invalid ranges.\n- **Operators:** only `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `=` may be followed by whitespace before the version. `~ 1.2.3` and `^ 1.2.3` are treated as invalid.\n- **`>*` and `<*`:** these match nothing, while `>=*`, `<=*` and `=*` match any non-prerelease version.\n- **Empty sets:** an empty comparator set, as in `1.0.0 ||` or a blank range, means \"any version\", like `*`.\n- **Leading zeros:** these are rejected in the numeric `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` parts but accepted in prerelease identifiers, because the spec doesn't forbid them there.\n- **Large numbers:** versions are compared with `BigInt`, so very large numbers work.\n\nThe new tests are in `test/satisfies.test.js`. They cover the spec's tables, version ordering, the prerelease rule, invalid input and the never-throws guarantee. I haven't committed anything.",
